# User-visible success and error messages

Every user-facing action that can succeed or fail must communicate the outcome in the UI.

## Required

- On failure: show a clear, human-readable error (title + short reason when useful). Never fail silently, swallow errors without UI, or early-return without feedback.
- On success: show confirmation when the user would otherwise be unsure the action worked (submit, create, upload, claim, stake, etc.).
- Prefer existing patterns (`FeedbackModal`, inline error banners, toasts) over inventing a new channel.
- Preserve specific server/AI reasons when they are already written for humans (e.g. proof rejection text). Do not replace them with a generic "Something went wrong" unless the raw message is technical.
- Async handlers passed into modals must be `await`ed so the modal can leave loading state and display errors.
- If a preconditions check blocks an action (missing wallet, missing id, wrong network), tell the user why.

## Avoid

- Empty `catch` blocks on user-triggered flows
- `if (!ready) return;` without setting an error or disabling the control with an explanation
- Clearing error state on close/retry without also handling the next failure
